1
00:00:01,030 --> 00:00:06,556
Fifteen minutes. That's how long it took
a quiet river in Zion to carry nearly 50

2
00:00:06,596 --> 00:00:10,220
times more water, with seven
people inside the canyon.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,275
September 14, 2015.

4
00:00:15,315 --> 00:00:17,995
A morning in Zion National Park.

5
00:00:18,035 --> 00:00:22,328
A group of seven canyoneers from
California and Nevada picks up

6
00:00:22,368 --> 00:00:26,220
a permit to descend a narrow
slot called Keyhole Canyon.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,276
At 2:22 that afternoon,
the National Weather

8
00:00:31,316 --> 00:00:34,065
Service issues a flash flood warning.

9
00:00:34,105 --> 00:00:36,200
The park closes its canyons.

10
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:42,640
Sometime between 3:30 and 4:30 that
afternoon, the group enters the slot.

11
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:48,300
Barely more than half an inch
of rain falls in about an hour.

12
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,330
And the North Fork of
the Virgin River goes

13
00:00:52,370 --> 00:00:55,985
from 55 cubic feet per second to 2,630.

14
00:00:56,025 --> 00:00:57,610
In fifteen minutes.

15
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:02,840
None of the seven came out alive.

16
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:08,640
More than 60 searchers spent over
1,000 hours looking for them.

17
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:11,699
High water and more rain
kept searchers out of

18
00:01:11,739 --> 00:01:14,425
the canyon's technical sections for days.

19
00:01:14,465 --> 00:01:17,161
The last of the seven was found that

20
00:01:17,201 --> 00:01:20,470
Thursday, downstream,
in Pine Creek Canyon.

21
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:24,930
And here's the part
that surprised us most.

22
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:28,000
The Park Service says a
flow that high happens

23
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,910
on this river about
once every three years.

24
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:37,036
That same afternoon, south of the
park, in the town of Hildale, a wall

25
00:01:37,076 --> 00:01:41,175
of water came out of another canyon
and swept away two vehicles.

26
00:01:41,215 --> 00:01:43,380
Thirteen women and children died.

27
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,520
Twenty people, in a few
hours, in one corner of Utah.

28
00:01:52,290 --> 00:01:56,580
This is the other side of one of
America's most-visited canyons.

29
00:01:56,620 --> 00:02:01,475
The beauty that draws nearly five
million people a year is made by the

30
00:02:01,515 --> 00:02:05,920
same water, and the same rock,
that can turn deadly in minutes.

31
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:20,780
To understand why Zion floods the way
it does, you have to look at the walls.

32
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:26,053
Zion's cliffs are Navajo
Sandstone, petrified

33
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:29,155
sand dunes more than 2,000 feet thick.

34
00:02:29,195 --> 00:02:33,505
Bare rock doesn't soak up a
sudden storm. It sheds it.

35
00:02:33,545 --> 00:02:36,497
Every side canyon becomes
a funnel, and the

36
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:39,320
water races toward the narrowest places.

37
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:45,805
The Virgin River drops 71 feet for
every mile it runs through the park.

38
00:02:45,845 --> 00:02:48,520
The Mississippi drops about an inch.

39
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:52,065
And this small river carries
about a million tons of

40
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:56,060
sediment out of Zion every
year, most of it during floods.

41
00:02:57,650 --> 00:03:00,983
Side canyons like this
one, Mystery Canyon, drop

42
00:03:01,023 --> 00:03:03,935
straight into the Narrows over waterfalls.

43
00:03:03,975 --> 00:03:06,580
Canyoneers rappel down them with ropes.

44
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:11,480
In other words, the floods
aren't an accident in Zion.

45
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,440
They are how the canyon was made.

46
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:22,046
The same flash floods that carved these
walls over millions of years are still at

47
00:03:22,086 --> 00:03:27,300
work every summer, when monsoon storms
build over the plateau in the afternoon.

48
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:36,300
The most famous hike in the
park is also the wettest.

49
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:41,942
The geologist Grove Karl Gilbert, who
came through here in 1872 with the

50
00:03:41,982 --> 00:03:47,035
Wheeler Survey, is credited with
giving it its name: the Narrows.

51
00:03:47,075 --> 00:03:49,180
In the Narrows, the trail is the river.

52
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:53,449
The canyon walls rise 1,000
feet and more, and in places

53
00:03:53,489 --> 00:03:57,300
the river between them is
only 20 to 30 feet wide.

54
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:02,340
We hiked it from the top,
down, in November 2021.

55
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:07,440
Cold water, slippery rocks, and walls
so high you lose sight of the sky.

56
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:13,820
In a slot this narrow, when the water
rises, there is often nowhere to climb.

57
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:20,471
So the park closes the Narrows when
the river runs above 150 cubic feet

58
00:04:20,511 --> 00:04:24,610
per second, and whenever a flash
flood warning is in effect.

59
00:04:26,490 --> 00:04:29,800
The danger is that the storm
doesn't have to be where you are.

60
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:35,400
Rain can fall miles away, out of sight,
and the flood arrives under a blue sky.

61
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:42,200
In July 1998, a flash flood in
the Narrows killed two hikers.

62
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:46,351
And in August 2022, a
29-year-old hiker from

63
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:50,125
Tucson, Jetal Agnihotri, was swept away.

64
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:55,290
Her body was found three days later,
about six river miles downstream.

65
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,461
Some of Zion's canyons are so tight
that the only way through is down:

66
00:05:07,501 --> 00:05:11,290
rappelling over waterfalls,
swimming the pools between them.

67
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:18,282
In July 1993, a church group from
Salt Lake City, three leaders

68
00:05:18,322 --> 00:05:22,750
and five teenage boys, set
out to descend Kolob Creek.

69
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,470
The water was much higher
than they expected.

70
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,433
One leader, Kim Ellis,
drowned in a whirlpool while

71
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:33,540
trying to rescue another
leader, David Fleischer.

72
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:38,310
Later, Fleischer was swept over
another waterfall. He drowned too.

73
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:44,325
The last adult and the five boys
climbed onto a ledge above the torrent.

74
00:05:44,365 --> 00:05:46,900
And they waited there. Four nights.

75
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:52,465
Rescuers finally reached
them and winched them out of

76
00:05:52,505 --> 00:05:55,725
the canyon, almost 1,500
feet, then flew them out.

77
00:05:55,765 --> 00:05:57,250
All six survived.

78
00:05:59,090 --> 00:06:03,900
Families later sued the Park Service and
the county water district, arguing the

79
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:08,995
group should have been warned about water
being released from a reservoir upstream.

80
00:06:09,035 --> 00:06:13,690
In Zion, the water you see isn't
always the water that's coming.

81
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:22,000
And sometimes the danger is invisible.

82
00:06:23,710 --> 00:06:27,415
On July 4, 2020, a dog
died about an hour after

83
00:06:27,455 --> 00:06:31,080
playing in the North Fork
of the Virgin River.

84
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,810
It had been eating floating mats of algae.

85
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:41,043
Park scientists found a cyanobacterium,
a microbe, producing a potent nerve

86
00:06:41,083 --> 00:06:46,090
toxin called anatoxin-a, at levels
far above what's considered safe.

87
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:52,047
Today the park monitors its
streams for toxic cyanobacteria,

88
00:06:52,087 --> 00:06:55,675
from the Narrows and the
Emerald Pools to the Subway.

89
00:06:55,715 --> 00:06:59,476
Don't drink the water untreated,
don't touch or swallow

90
00:06:59,516 --> 00:07:03,110
the algae, and never let a
dog drink from the river.

91
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:11,385
The strange twist is that the same family
of microbes is everywhere in this desert.

92
00:07:11,425 --> 00:07:16,263
Cyanobacteria help form the dark
crust on Zion's soil, the living skin

93
00:07:16,303 --> 00:07:20,445
that holds the sand together
and keeps it from blowing away.

94
00:07:20,485 --> 00:07:23,320
In the soil, they build the desert.

95
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:27,310
In the river, in the wrong
conditions, they turn toxic.

96
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,800
Not all of Zion's danger comes from water.

97
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,340
Some of it comes from height.

98
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:42,363
In 1916, a visiting
minister named a narrow

99
00:07:42,403 --> 00:07:45,635
fin of rock standing
high above the canyon.

100
00:07:45,675 --> 00:07:49,265
Only an angel, the story
goes, could land on it.

101
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:51,940
The name stuck: Angels Landing.

102
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:56,970
Ten years later, the park
built a trail to the top.

103
00:07:57,010 --> 00:08:02,107
Under the park's acting superintendent,
crews cut it into the cliff with hand

104
00:08:02,147 --> 00:08:07,350
tools, including the tight stack of
switchbacks still called Walter's Wiggles.

105
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:13,805
The last half mile follows a spine of
rock with sheer drops on both sides.

106
00:08:13,845 --> 00:08:16,709
For much of that stretch,
the only thing to

107
00:08:16,749 --> 00:08:19,720
hold on to is a chain
bolted into the stone.

108
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:23,640
We climbed it in 2021.

109
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:27,410
The top stands nearly 1,500
feet above the river.

110
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:35,077
Researchers count at least 17
deaths at Angels Landing over

111
00:08:35,117 --> 00:08:38,855
the decades, more than a
dozen of them since 2000.

112
00:08:38,895 --> 00:08:43,616
One researcher who studies deaths in
national parks said that if it isn't

113
00:08:43,656 --> 00:08:47,700
the most dangerous trail in
America, it's one of the top five.

114
00:08:49,630 --> 00:08:52,805
Many of the falls didn't
even happen on the chains.

115
00:08:52,845 --> 00:08:57,725
They happened before or after them,
on stretches where people felt safe.

116
00:08:57,765 --> 00:09:03,360
The most recent death, in April 2026,
was a fall from the chained section.

117
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:08,562
By the late 2010s, so many
people were lining up on

118
00:09:08,602 --> 00:09:11,515
the chains that the
park changed the rules.

119
00:09:11,555 --> 00:09:15,244
Since April 2022, you need
a permit to climb Angels

120
00:09:15,284 --> 00:09:18,940
Landing, and the permits
are given out by lottery.

121
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:22,931
The park says the goal was to reduce

122
00:09:22,971 --> 00:09:25,745
crowding and make the
climb fairer and safer.

123
00:09:25,785 --> 00:09:31,065
In the first two years, it
issued nearly 400,000 permits.

124
00:09:31,105 --> 00:09:35,640
On the busiest days, the odds of
winning were as low as 8 percent.

125
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,310
The crowds left other traces, too.

126
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:46,226
In 2021, park crews hauled almost
five tons of human waste off the

127
00:09:46,266 --> 00:09:50,660
toilets at Scout Lookout, the
saddle just below the chains.

128
00:09:56,290 --> 00:10:00,635
In 1920, fewer than 4,000
people visited Zion.

129
00:10:00,675 --> 00:10:04,820
In 2021, more than five
million did, a record.

130
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:09,585
Most of them leave with
nothing but photographs.

131
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:15,123
But the rangers here answer hundreds of
emergency calls every year, because the

132
00:10:15,163 --> 00:10:20,420
things that make Zion beautiful, the
water and the rock, never stop moving.

133
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:26,605
And sometimes, they move all at once.

134
00:10:26,645 --> 00:10:31,367
The flat valley floor where the park's
lodge stands today is the bottom of a

135
00:10:31,407 --> 00:10:35,480
lake, left behind when a mountainside
collapsed into this canyon.

136
00:10:37,230 --> 00:10:39,130
That's the next chapter. See you there.

137
00:10:40,964 --> 00:10:45,065
The hotel in the middle of Zion
Canyon sits on the bottom of a

138
00:10:45,105 --> 00:10:48,644
lake, a lake that vanished
more than 4,000 years ago.

139
00:10:50,404 --> 00:10:57,014
Where this lawn is now, the water once
stood about 350 to 380 feet deep.

140
00:10:58,854 --> 00:11:00,834
And that lake was made in about a minute.

141
00:11:04,634 --> 00:11:09,421
About 4,800 years ago, a huge part
of the canyon wall near a peak

142
00:11:09,461 --> 00:11:13,729
called the Sentinel broke loose
and fell into Zion Canyon.

143
00:11:13,769 --> 00:11:18,054
Models suggest it reached
nearly 200 miles per hour.

144
00:11:18,094 --> 00:11:20,864
One of the geologists
who studied it put it

145
00:11:20,904 --> 00:11:23,844
simply: by a minute, it
was pretty much done.

146
00:11:25,644 --> 00:11:28,444
The rubble dammed the
Virgin River, and behind

147
00:11:28,484 --> 00:11:30,914
it grew a lake that some call Lake Zion.

148
00:11:30,954 --> 00:11:33,849
It lasted about 700 years.

149
00:11:33,889 --> 00:11:38,805
When it finally filled with sand and
mud, it left the flat green valley

150
00:11:38,845 --> 00:11:43,034
floor that millions of visitors
now walk, ride and sleep on.

151
00:11:44,864 --> 00:11:50,034
The same geologist called it a minute
with up to 10,000 years of consequences.

152
00:11:53,214 --> 00:11:55,194
And the mountain isn't finished.

153
00:11:55,234 --> 00:11:59,428
In 1995, part of that
ancient slide moved again,

154
00:11:59,468 --> 00:12:03,614
and about 430 people were
cut off at the lodge.

155
00:12:05,254 --> 00:12:08,534
This is America's National
Parks: The Hidden History.

156
00:12:08,574 --> 00:12:10,209
Zion, Part Two.

157
00:12:10,249 --> 00:12:11,824
The Day the Mountain Fell.

158
00:12:22,084 --> 00:12:27,779
The walls of Zion Canyon rise 2,000
to 3,000 feet above the canyon floor.

159
00:12:27,819 --> 00:12:32,464
The Park Service counts them among the
highest sandstone cliffs on Earth.

160
00:12:34,304 --> 00:12:38,022
But in the middle of the canyon,
the floor goes strangely

161
00:12:38,062 --> 00:12:41,319
flat, wide enough for
meadows, a road and a hotel.

162
00:12:41,359 --> 00:12:46,914
In 2016, a team from the University
of Utah published the explanation.

163
00:12:48,754 --> 00:12:52,978
They dated the slide using
beryllium-ten, a rare form of the

164
00:12:53,018 --> 00:12:57,314
element that slowly builds up
in rock exposed to cosmic rays.

165
00:12:57,354 --> 00:13:01,962
Measured in boulders the
slide left behind, it gave

166
00:13:02,002 --> 00:13:06,194
an age of about 4,800
years, give or take 400.

167
00:13:08,004 --> 00:13:12,459
What fell was enormous: about
10 billion cubic feet of rock.

168
00:13:12,499 --> 00:13:16,490
To rebuild that missing
mountainside, the researchers said,

169
00:13:16,530 --> 00:13:20,014
you would need 90 times the
concrete in Hoover Dam.

170
00:13:21,834 --> 00:13:25,830
It was roughly 5 times bigger
than the largest landslides in

171
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:29,704
North America's recorded
history, not counting volcanoes.

172
00:13:31,354 --> 00:13:35,739
Models suggest it averaged
about 112 miles per hour.

173
00:13:35,779 --> 00:13:39,704
The main mass crossed the
canyon in about 20 seconds.

174
00:13:39,744 --> 00:13:42,504
The debris kept spreading
for about 30 more.

175
00:13:44,294 --> 00:13:49,242
When the dust settled, the canyon
was blocked from wall to wall by a

176
00:13:49,282 --> 00:13:53,684
dam about 2 miles long and, in
places, about 650 feet thick.

177
00:14:01,024 --> 00:14:03,299
The Virgin River had nowhere to go.

178
00:14:03,339 --> 00:14:07,434
It backed up behind the rubble,
and the canyon began to fill.

179
00:14:07,474 --> 00:14:10,417
The researchers estimate
the lake reached its

180
00:14:10,457 --> 00:14:13,374
first high point within
about 5 to 10 years.

181
00:14:14,994 --> 00:14:16,874
Picture this view as water.

182
00:14:16,914 --> 00:14:19,853
At its highest, the
lake covered almost 2.5

183
00:14:19,893 --> 00:14:23,109
square miles and reached
almost to the Narrows.

184
00:14:23,149 --> 00:14:26,802
Over the spot where Zion Lodge stands

185
00:14:26,842 --> 00:14:30,934
today, it was about 350 to 380 feet deep.

186
00:14:32,764 --> 00:14:35,214
Then the water found a way over the top.

187
00:14:35,254 --> 00:14:40,144
The lake cut into the crest of its dam
and dropped to a lower, steadier level.

188
00:14:40,184 --> 00:14:44,885
It shrank to about 1.2 square
miles, but it still reached more

189
00:14:44,925 --> 00:14:49,054
than 4 miles up the canyon,
to the Temple of Sinawava.

190
00:14:50,844 --> 00:14:54,119
And then, slowly, the
river buried its own lake.

191
00:14:54,159 --> 00:14:57,023
Year after year it carried
sand and mud down the

192
00:14:57,063 --> 00:14:59,664
canyon and dropped them
in the still water.

193
00:15:01,384 --> 00:15:08,144
About 700 years after the mountain fell,
around 4,100 years ago, the lake was full.

194
00:15:08,184 --> 00:15:10,304
Not of water. Of sediment.

195
00:15:12,084 --> 00:15:14,704
That is the flat floor of Zion Canyon.

196
00:15:14,744 --> 00:15:18,296
The meadows, the cottonwoods,
the road and the lodge

197
00:15:18,336 --> 00:15:21,404
lawn all sit on a lake
that filled itself in.

198
00:15:21,444 --> 00:15:25,836
Jeff Moore, one of the University
of Utah geologists behind the

199
00:15:25,876 --> 00:15:29,604
study, called it creating
paradise through cataclysm.

200
00:15:31,404 --> 00:15:34,884
The river has been eating
away at the dam ever since.

201
00:15:34,924 --> 00:15:39,185
The researchers estimate that
about 45 percent of the slide's

202
00:15:39,225 --> 00:15:43,174
debris is already gone, carried
off by the Virgin River.

203
00:15:50,424 --> 00:15:54,044
Thousands of years later, people
found that flat floor useful.

204
00:15:55,754 --> 00:16:00,075
In 1917, the first road for
cars reached deep into Zion

205
00:16:00,115 --> 00:16:04,039
Canyon, and a tent camp
opened on the old lakebed.

206
00:16:04,079 --> 00:16:08,344
It had 10 tent cabins, right
where the lodge stands today.

207
00:16:10,174 --> 00:16:15,459
In the early 1920s, the Union Pacific
Railroad saw a business opportunity.

208
00:16:15,499 --> 00:16:20,003
A rail spur reached Cedar
City, Utah, in 1923, and the

209
00:16:20,043 --> 00:16:25,304
railroad's Utah Parks Company
needed a hotel inside the canyon.

210
00:16:25,344 --> 00:16:28,724
The company hired Gilbert
Stanley Underwood, an

211
00:16:28,764 --> 00:16:32,799
architect who designed lodges
in several national parks.

212
00:16:32,839 --> 00:16:36,174
Zion Lodge was finished in May 1925.

213
00:16:38,024 --> 00:16:39,794
Here's the detail we love.

214
00:16:39,834 --> 00:16:43,662
The lodge was built with
265,000 board feet of

215
00:16:43,702 --> 00:16:47,699
lumber, brought down from
the plateau on a wire.

216
00:16:47,739 --> 00:16:51,976
A tramway called the Cable Mountain
Draw Works lowered it more

217
00:16:52,016 --> 00:16:56,224
than 2,000 vertical feet, down
the cliff to the canyon floor.

218
00:16:58,064 --> 00:17:01,494
Hold on to that name, Cable
Mountain. We'll come back to it.

219
00:17:03,194 --> 00:17:07,844
For more than 40 years, Underwood's
lodge stood on the lakebed.

220
00:17:07,884 --> 00:17:13,454
Then, on January 26, 1966, the
main lodge building burned.

221
00:17:15,314 --> 00:17:17,214
The replacement went up fast.

222
00:17:17,254 --> 00:17:22,934
A new, prefabricated building was
operating just 108 days after the fire.

223
00:17:24,764 --> 00:17:30,519
In 1990, the lodge was restored to look
more like Underwood's original design.

224
00:17:30,559 --> 00:17:34,934
That's the lodge you see today,
standing in the middle of the old lake.

225
00:17:36,764 --> 00:17:39,584
And in 1995, it became an island.

226
00:17:46,864 --> 00:17:52,814
On the night of April 12, 1995, part
of the old Sentinel slide moved again.

227
00:17:54,664 --> 00:17:59,718
A landslide more than 500 feet
long, over 100,000 cubic yards of

228
00:17:59,758 --> 00:18:04,254
rock and soil, slid into the
Virgin River and blocked it.

229
00:18:04,294 --> 00:18:07,234
A small lake began to form behind it.

230
00:18:07,274 --> 00:18:12,024
And the river, pushed out of its bed, cut
a new path straight through the road.

231
00:18:13,884 --> 00:18:17,959
Rangers estimated that 600
feet of road was destroyed.

232
00:18:17,999 --> 00:18:21,229
It was the only road in
and out of the canyon.

233
00:18:21,269 --> 00:18:25,696
Upstream, at Zion Lodge,
about 430 people, guests and

234
00:18:25,736 --> 00:18:30,204
employees, by the Park
Service's count, were cut off.

235
00:18:32,074 --> 00:18:36,804
Crews carved an emergency detour
road out of the canyon's east wall.

236
00:18:36,844 --> 00:18:38,834
It took about 22 hours.

237
00:18:38,874 --> 00:18:44,554
On the night of April 13, the people stuck
at the lodge were finally able to leave.

238
00:18:46,414 --> 00:18:49,424
Three years later, the
old slide was hit again.

239
00:18:49,464 --> 00:18:55,359
In 1998, a flash flood sent the
river from 200 to 4,500 cubic feet

240
00:18:55,399 --> 00:19:00,524
per second, and it damaged the
scenic drive at the slide.

241
00:19:02,354 --> 00:19:05,389
The dam from 4,800 years
ago is still there,

242
00:19:05,429 --> 00:19:08,154
and the river is still cutting into it.

243
00:19:15,434 --> 00:19:17,374
The Park Service puts it plainly.

244
00:19:17,414 --> 00:19:21,158
In Zion, rocks fall, frequently,
and it is impossible to

245
00:19:21,198 --> 00:19:24,374
predict when or where the
next one will happen.

246
00:19:26,124 --> 00:19:29,459
Water seeps into cracks and
freezes, wedging them apart.

247
00:19:29,499 --> 00:19:32,611
The sun heats the rock
by day and it cools at

248
00:19:32,651 --> 00:19:36,114
night, expanding and
shrinking until it fractures.

249
00:19:36,154 --> 00:19:38,719
Floods undercut the cliffs from below.

250
00:19:38,759 --> 00:19:42,954
And sometimes, as the park
itself says, rocks just fall.

251
00:19:44,764 --> 00:19:49,427
On August 1, 1968, about 5,000
tons of rock came down at

252
00:19:49,467 --> 00:19:53,414
the end of the trail that
leads to the Narrows.

253
00:19:55,094 --> 00:20:00,505
Then, on the evening of August 24,
2019, a huge slab of sandstone

254
00:20:00,545 --> 00:20:05,704
broke off Cable Mountain, about
3,000 feet above Weeping Rock.

255
00:20:05,744 --> 00:20:10,864
Yes, the same Cable Mountain that once
lowered the lumber for the lodge.

256
00:20:12,564 --> 00:20:17,539
More than 435,000 cubic
feet of debris came down.

257
00:20:17,579 --> 00:20:21,784
Three visitors were hurt, and
one was taken to the hospital.

258
00:20:21,824 --> 00:20:25,199
Part of the East Rim Trail
was buried under what

259
00:20:25,239 --> 00:20:28,654
geologists classified as
a small rock avalanche.

260
00:20:30,274 --> 00:20:33,849
The Weeping Rock Trail
stayed closed for 3 years.

261
00:20:33,889 --> 00:20:36,764
It reopened in November 2022.

262
00:20:36,804 --> 00:20:42,734
A year later, another slab fell near the
same spot, and the trail closed again.

263
00:20:42,774 --> 00:20:45,894
It didn't reopen until September 2025.

264
00:20:47,764 --> 00:20:52,537
After that second fall, the park's
superintendent, Jeff Bradybaugh, said it

265
00:20:52,577 --> 00:20:57,134
well: rockfall can happen at any time
in this highly erosive landscape.

266
00:20:58,934 --> 00:21:03,644
And yet, according to the Park Service,
no visitor has ever been killed by a

267
00:21:03,684 --> 00:21:08,684
rockfall in Zion, though people have been
hurt, and there have been close calls.

268
00:21:15,784 --> 00:21:19,459
Today, mule deer graze in the
meadows on the old lakebed.

269
00:21:19,499 --> 00:21:23,072
But two Oregon State
University ecologists argue

270
00:21:23,112 --> 00:21:26,724
that even this quiet scene
has a hidden history.

271
00:21:28,524 --> 00:21:34,349
By their count, in 1924, about
8,400 people visited Zion.

272
00:21:34,389 --> 00:21:38,719
Ten years later, almost 69,000 did.

273
00:21:38,759 --> 00:21:44,604
A Park Service report from 1939 said the
crowds tended to drive out the cougars.

274
00:21:46,224 --> 00:21:50,973
Without cougars, the researchers
say, the canyon's deer

275
00:21:51,013 --> 00:21:55,849
boomed, from fewer than 80 in
1930 to about 600 by 1942.

276
00:21:55,889 --> 00:22:00,085
Between 1938 and 1947, the Park Service

277
00:22:00,125 --> 00:22:04,904
killed or removed 780
deer from Zion Canyon.

278
00:22:06,754 --> 00:22:08,919
And the deer ate the young cottonwoods.

279
00:22:08,959 --> 00:22:13,716
In Zion Canyon, the researchers
found only about 23 cottonwoods

280
00:22:13,756 --> 00:22:17,904
per kilometer of stream that
had taken root after 1940.

281
00:22:17,944 --> 00:22:23,459
In nearby North Creek, where the
cougars stayed, there were almost 900.

282
00:22:23,499 --> 00:22:27,324
Without new trees holding the
soil, the banks wore away.

283
00:22:29,134 --> 00:22:35,054
It is the authors' hypothesis, and ideas
like this are debated among ecologists.

284
00:22:35,094 --> 00:22:38,514
But it's a reminder that the
lakebed is still changing too.

285
00:22:40,234 --> 00:22:44,379
So the next time you stand on the
lawn at Zion Lodge, look down.

286
00:22:44,419 --> 00:22:48,401
You are standing on the mud and
sand of a lake that some call Lake

287
00:22:48,441 --> 00:22:52,464
Zion, a lake born from a mountainside
that fell in about a minute.

288
00:22:54,264 --> 00:22:58,450
Could it happen again? Jeff Moore
chose his words carefully: we have no

289
00:22:58,490 --> 00:23:01,274
evidence that something
like this is imminent.

290
00:23:01,314 --> 00:23:05,814
But this canyon is still being
built, one falling rock at a time.

291
00:23:07,654 --> 00:23:11,734
Building anything in this canyon has
always meant a fight with the rock.

292
00:23:11,774 --> 00:23:14,489
And the boldest fight of all was a road.

293
00:23:14,529 --> 00:23:18,134
In the late 1920s, crews
set out to blast a tunnel

294
00:23:18,174 --> 00:23:21,674
more than a mile long
through a sandstone cliff.

295
00:23:23,554 --> 00:23:26,362
How they did it, and how
this canyon became a

296
00:23:26,402 --> 00:23:29,124
national park at all,
is our final chapter.

297
00:23:30,991 --> 00:23:39,191
Two crews started at opposite ends of a
mile of solid sandstone, and they dug
toward each other in the dark.

298
00:23:39,591 --> 00:23:43,347
They used a hundred and forty-six tons of
dynamite.

299
00:23:43,397 --> 00:23:48,049
There was no GPS and no laser, only
surveyors, string and math.

300
00:23:48,099 --> 00:23:58,571
And when the two bores finally broke
through into each other, a newspaper
reported that nowhere were they more than
three inches out of line.

301
00:23:58,961 --> 00:24:11,461
It is the Zion–Mount Carmel tunnel, and to
understand why anyone would try to build
it, you have to start with a painter who
showed the world a canyon it did not
believe.

302
00:24:11,871 --> 00:24:16,372
This is America's National Parks: The
Hidden History.

303
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:17,832
Zion, Part Three.

304
00:24:17,882 --> 00:24:19,721
The Impossible Tunnel.

305
00:24:24,121 --> 00:24:32,050
For most of the nineteenth century, Zion
Canyon was known mostly to the farming
towns of southern Utah.

306
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:35,071
To most of the country, it was a rumor.

307
00:24:35,471 --> 00:24:41,752
In the summer of nineteen oh three, a
painter named Frederick Dellenbaugh came
to see it.

308
00:24:41,802 --> 00:24:43,957
He was no stranger to the West.

309
00:24:44,007 --> 00:24:50,288
As a young man he had traveled with John
Wesley Powell, and he had seen a lot of
canyons.

310
00:24:50,338 --> 00:24:51,782
This one stopped him.

311
00:24:51,832 --> 00:24:57,401
In his words, never before had such a
naked mountain of rock entered our minds.

312
00:24:57,791 --> 00:24:58,777
He painted it.

313
00:24:58,827 --> 00:25:04,330
His canvases of Zion were shown at the
nineteen oh four World's Fair in St.

314
00:25:04,380 --> 00:25:11,511
Louis, and he wrote about the canyon for
Scribner's Magazine, calling it a new
valley of wonders.

315
00:25:11,921 --> 00:25:17,621
According to the National Park Service,
the visitors who saw those paintings in
St.

316
00:25:17,671 --> 00:25:21,154
Louis doubted that a place like that could
be real.

317
00:25:21,204 --> 00:25:23,163
It was a hard thing to prove.

318
00:25:23,213 --> 00:25:29,121
There was no highway to the canyon, and
for most people, the paint was the only
proof.

319
00:25:33,511 --> 00:25:40,211
Back in Utah, people who actually lived
near it started asking for official
protection.

320
00:25:40,611 --> 00:25:47,232
In nineteen oh eight, eight ranchers from
southern Utah asked the government for a
land survey.

321
00:25:47,282 --> 00:25:51,095
The General Land Office sent a surveyor
named Leo Snow.

322
00:25:51,145 --> 00:25:59,522
He surveyed the canyon and wrote that it
was surpassed in grandeur only by the view
of the Grand Canyon of the Colorado.

323
00:25:59,572 --> 00:26:05,491
His recommendation was short: Zion Canyon,
he said, ought to be made a national park.

324
00:26:05,891 --> 00:26:16,328
On July thirty-first, nineteen oh nine,
President William Howard Taft signed a
proclamation that created Mukuntuweap
National Monument.

325
00:26:16,378 --> 00:26:23,941
It was small, about fifteen thousand
acres, a strip roughly half a mile wide
and eight miles long.

326
00:26:24,341 --> 00:26:25,958
The name didn't last.

327
00:26:26,008 --> 00:26:37,391
It was unpopular with local people, and in
nineteen eighteen the monument was renamed
Zion and enlarged to more than seventy-six
thousand acres.

328
00:26:37,791 --> 00:26:48,241
And on November nineteenth, nineteen
nineteen, Congress made Zion a national
park, Utah's first, signed by President
Woodrow Wilson.

329
00:26:48,641 --> 00:26:51,207
But a park on paper is not a park.

330
00:26:51,257 --> 00:26:56,363
In nineteen twenty, about thirty-seven
hundred people visited Zion.

331
00:26:56,413 --> 00:27:02,211
There was a dirt road, a tent camp, and no
way to get here that wasn't hard.

332
00:27:06,611 --> 00:27:10,804
Some of the people who saw the opportunity
were railroad men.

333
00:27:10,854 --> 00:27:18,874
Union Pacific and its subsidiary, the Utah
Parks Company, wanted tourists to ride
their trains to the western parks.

334
00:27:18,924 --> 00:27:32,091
A rail spur reached Cedar City, Utah, in
nineteen twenty-three, and one historian
counts about one point seven million
dollars spent on improvements in nineteen
twenty-three and twenty-four.

335
00:27:32,491 --> 00:27:36,929
In June nineteen twenty-three, the biggest
tourist of all arrived.

336
00:27:36,979 --> 00:27:43,252
President Warren Harding came to Cedar
City, Utah, by train, to thousands of
cheering locals.

337
00:27:43,302 --> 00:27:48,148
Sheriffs rounded up about seventy-five
horses to ride him up the canyon.

338
00:27:48,198 --> 00:27:55,695
According to a Deseret News account, the
president wore a cowboy hat, a kerchief
and a pair of oversized chaps.

339
00:27:55,745 --> 00:28:00,591
His wife, Florence, said she wouldn't have
missed the trip for anything.

340
00:28:00,991 --> 00:28:03,773
Five weeks later, Harding was dead.

341
00:28:03,823 --> 00:28:08,708
He died in San Francisco on August second,
still on the trip.

342
00:28:08,758 --> 00:28:11,297
But the visit had done its work.

343
00:28:11,347 --> 00:28:12,591
Zion was famous.

344
00:28:12,991 --> 00:28:15,724
Visitors came by train and by bus.

345
00:28:15,774 --> 00:28:25,711
By nineteen thirty, nearly fifty-five
thousand people visited in a year, about
fifteen times as many as ten years
earlier.

346
00:28:26,111 --> 00:28:29,211
And that created a new problem.

347
00:28:33,611 --> 00:28:36,005
Zion Canyon is a box canyon.

348
00:28:36,055 --> 00:28:40,631
The road went in, and the road came out
the same way.

349
00:28:41,041 --> 00:28:47,397
Bryce Canyon and the other wonders of the
plateau country lay beyond the cliff wall.

350
00:28:47,447 --> 00:28:54,641
To drive from Zion to Bryce, you had to go
the long way around, a hundred and forty-
nine miles.

351
00:28:55,041 --> 00:29:00,343
There was an older way over that wall, and
you have already heard about it.

352
00:29:00,393 --> 00:29:10,619
Around nineteen hundred, a logger named
David Flanigan strung about fifty thousand
feet of wire up the east rim to lower
lumber into the canyon.

353
00:29:10,669 --> 00:29:15,971
Before that, a wagon trip to bring lumber
down could take a week or longer.

354
00:29:16,361 --> 00:29:25,081
And according to the Park Service, a
family dog named Darkey once rode that
wire two thousand feet down the mountain.

355
00:29:25,491 --> 00:29:27,320
A dog could do it.

356
00:29:27,370 --> 00:29:28,991
A car could not.

357
00:29:33,391 --> 00:29:48,461
So the Park Service, the state of Utah and
the Bureau of Public Roads agreed to build
something that sounded almost absurd: a
road up the side of the canyon, and a
tunnel through the cliff above it, to the
plateau.

358
00:29:48,861 --> 00:29:53,074
It took more than four years of surveys
just to find a route.

359
00:29:53,124 --> 00:29:59,504
A local stockman, John Winder, is credited
with showing the surveyors the way up Pine
Creek.

360
00:29:59,554 --> 00:30:07,261
When they were done, the road to Bryce
Canyon would shrink from a hundred and
forty-nine miles to eighty-eight.

361
00:30:07,661 --> 00:30:11,573
Construction began in September nineteen
twenty-seven.

362
00:30:11,623 --> 00:30:15,608
A Nevada construction company from Fallon
took the job.

363
00:30:15,658 --> 00:30:21,111
About two hundred workers and their
families lived in a camp near the work.

364
00:30:21,511 --> 00:30:27,612
The crews started by blasting openings in
the cliff face, windows high above Pine
Creek.

365
00:30:27,662 --> 00:30:32,086
Through them, they hauled out the rock and
let in air and light.

366
00:30:32,136 --> 00:30:39,775
Then they bored the tunnel in two
directions, toward each other, and widened
it with dynamite and air shovels.

367
00:30:39,825 --> 00:30:42,361
Several times, parts of it collapsed.

368
00:30:42,771 --> 00:30:44,503
The work was dangerous.

369
00:30:44,553 --> 00:30:52,248
One local history names two men who died:
a crew boss, Johnny Morrison, and a man
named Mac McClain.

370
00:30:52,298 --> 00:31:01,621
The Park Service pages don't mention
deaths, so we say it carefully: at least
two men reportedly died building this
road.

371
00:31:02,011 --> 00:31:09,261
When the two bores met, a newspaper
reported, they were nowhere more than
three inches out of line.

372
00:31:09,311 --> 00:31:13,611
After a mile of rock, that was close
enough to shake hands.

373
00:31:18,011 --> 00:31:22,169
The tunnel was dedicated on July fourth,
nineteen thirty.

374
00:31:22,219 --> 00:31:25,122
Utah's governor, George Dern, was there.

375
00:31:25,172 --> 00:31:32,356
A prayer was offered by the president of
the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day
Saints, Heber J.

376
00:31:32,406 --> 00:31:32,799
Grant.

377
00:31:32,849 --> 00:31:41,141
Visiting governors from a governors'
conference came to see it, and the
ceremony was broadcast live on the radio.

378
00:31:41,541 --> 00:31:50,344
By the numbers, it was one point one mile
long, twenty-two feet wide and sixteen
feet high, with six windows cut into the
cliff.

379
00:31:50,394 --> 00:31:54,149
The tunnel alone had cost about half a
million dollars.

380
00:31:54,199 --> 00:31:59,889
The whole highway cost closer to two
million, at the start of the Great
Depression.

381
00:31:59,939 --> 00:32:07,221
At the time, the National Park Service
called it the longest vehicular tunnel in
the national park system.

382
00:32:07,621 --> 00:32:08,371
It worked.

383
00:32:08,421 --> 00:32:10,771
Zion was no longer a dead end.

384
00:32:10,821 --> 00:32:15,891
Before the tunnel, the park had about a
thousand visitors a day.

385
00:32:15,941 --> 00:32:18,371
It was about to see a lot more.

386
00:32:22,771 --> 00:32:26,659
The tunnel is still there, and it is still
in use.

387
00:32:26,709 --> 00:32:35,401
In two thousand twelve, the American
Society of Civil Engineers named it a
historic civil engineering landmark.

388
00:32:35,791 --> 00:32:37,560
Not everything has lasted.

389
00:32:37,610 --> 00:32:42,388
One of the windows was closed because
seepage turned the road to ice.

390
00:32:42,438 --> 00:32:48,686
Another was sealed by a rockslide in
nineteen fifty-eight, and two more were
closed later.

391
00:32:48,736 --> 00:32:59,671
The same mountain that the tunnel crosses
keeps working on it, which is the same
story as the lakebed, the lodge and the
trail: this canyon is always moving.

392
00:33:00,061 --> 00:33:08,967
And because it was built for nineteen-
twenties cars, today's biggest vehicles
can't squeeze through alongside traffic.

393
00:33:09,017 --> 00:33:17,391
Anything over eleven feet four inches
tall, or seven feet ten wide, needs a
permit and one-way traffic control.

394
00:33:21,791 --> 00:33:29,284
Today, Zion sees nearly five million
visits a year, the second most visited
national park in America.

395
00:33:29,334 --> 00:33:36,080
Every one of them is looking at something
a painter once swore was real, and a crowd
in St.

396
00:33:36,130 --> 00:33:38,321
Louis could not quite believe.

397
00:33:38,711 --> 00:33:50,728
From a mountain that fell in a minute, to
a lake that filled itself in, to a tunnel
that met in the middle, Zion has always
been a place that asks a lot of the people
who love it.

398
00:33:50,778 --> 00:33:53,847
And somehow it keeps giving them paradise
anyway.

